# The buzz under a thumb on the pad is the controller's own, and it is off here.
#
# Moving across the touchpad makes it tick. That is not something this desktop
# sends: with force feedback disabled and every path a program could use held
# under a kernel probe -- evdev effects, hidraw writes, feature reports, and
# the wire itself -- nothing left this machine at all while it was buzzing. The
# right controller does it in its own firmware, at whatever `hid-lenovo-go` has
# it set to, and what it leaves the factory at is on, and high.
#
# Which of the controller's three HID interfaces carries the settings group
# changes as the controllers come and go, so the group itself is what is
# matched on here rather than the number in the name.
#
# The file is then handed to the desktop user, because `controller-profile`
# runs in their session and turns it back on for `game`: a tick under a thumb
# is feedback for a pointer, and a game is the one place here that might want
# to be felt. sysfs carries no ACL -- setfacl answers "Operation not supported"
# on it -- so this is a chown, and not the uaccess and setfacl that
# 91-console-touchpad.rules needs for a device node. `@user@` is the mark
# `console apply` fills in, the same as there.
SUBSYSTEM=="hid", DRIVER=="hid-lenovo-go", ATTR{touchpad/vibration_enabled}=="?*", \
  ATTR{touchpad/vibration_enabled}="false", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/chown @user@ %S%p/touchpad/vibration_enabled"
